# Prelude No. 6 in D minor, Well-Tempered Clavier I, BWV 851 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a perpetual motion. The right hand runs in triplet
# sixteenths, broken chords rocking over the bar, while the left hand
# walks in steady eighths: first a drumming D pedal, then a bass line
# that steps down through the sequences. Urgent and restless, then a
# long tonic pedal, a diminished-seventh flourish and a quiet D major
# close.
#
# Time signature and bars: 4/4 (triplet sixteenths, six to the beat),
# 26 bars, no upbeat; the MIDI has the same 26 bars. Bar numbers below
# follow the MIDI; the bar-number comments in the Mutopia source
# (%5, %10, ...) stand before the bar after the one they name.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 70 (seven notes a second in the right hand), 104
# beats, about 1:31 with the close. Track times (MusicBrainz track
# lists): Angela Hewitt (Hyperion, 1998) 1:42, about 61; Edwin Fischer
# 1:25 and Sviatoslav Richter 1:26, about 73; Tureck 1:22 (older set),
# 1:12 (2002 issue), about 76 and 87; Friedrich Gulda 1:20 and Glenn
# Gould 1:19, about 79. 70 sits between Hewitt and Richter: it keeps the
# urgency, and the triplets do not smear on the chip. The Mutopia MIDI
# has 66.
#
# Structure (from the Mutopia score, checked against the MIDI; the key
# plan is my reading):
#   1-2    the triplet figure over a drumming D pedal in eighths.
#   3-9    the bass walks; a sequence that reaches the dominant,
#          A minor, around bar 9.
#   10-13  the sequence presses on through G minor and back.
#   14-15  the hands share the figure (the left hand takes triplets).
#   16-21  a tonic pedal: the left hand alternates the low D with
#          C-sharp, C, B-flat above it, under the running figure.
#   22-23  the bass divides into two lines; the motion slows.
#   24     the right hand alone, a diminished-seventh flourish.
#   25-26  the cadence, on a D major chord with a fermata.
#
# Range: the chip's lowest note is about B2, and the walking bass goes
# well below it (G2, F2, E2, D2, C2), so many bass notes are lifted an
# octave and the line jumps where Bach's steps smoothly. Nothing can be
# done about that here; the dynamics follow the harmony instead.

tempo 70

voice mid 0          # a running top line and a walking bass

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-9: the figure over the pedal, the sequence
                     # out to A minor
dyn 10 mp            # bars 10-13: after the A minor arrival, the
                     # sequence continues on a quieter manual
dyn 14 mf            # bars 14-15: the hands share the figure
ramp 16-21 f         # bars 16-21: the tonic pedal, the one long build
dyn 22 mf            # bars 22-23: the bass divides, a breath
dyn 24 f             # bar 24: the diminished-seventh flourish
dyn 25 mf            # bars 25-26: the cadence and the D major close

broaden 24 0.94      # a little room for the flourish
rit 2 0.6            # bars 25-26 into the final chord
